WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.269 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  huge b-52s flew up to 300 sorties a day  against suspected communist positions  [Music]  for nine years the united states  conducted one of the biggest bombing  campaigns in the history of the world  against the country pretty much no  american had any idea they were fighting  [Music]  the country they were targeting is not  one you've heard a lot about  it's not japan or korea or vietnam or  iraq  it's a country that is totally unknown  to the vast majority of people  this is the story of laos  on average bombs were dropped here every  eight minutes for nine years  randomly across fields and forests and  villages craters from the blasts still  scar the landscape about 80 million  undetonated bombs remain in the country  the american public needs to know what's  happening here that this is what their  country has has done and left behind  [Music]  laos is located right here in inland  southeast asia north of cambodia and  west of vietnam  historically of all these countries laos  was the poorest the least populated and  the least influential  since the end of the 19th century  vietnam cambodian laos had been french  possessions grouped together as french  indochina  but the decades after the second world  war saw a liberation struggle against  european colonialism  the year is 1954 the once mighty french  colonial empire in southeast asia is  doing  so along with cambodian vietnam laos  became an independent nation  but like its two sister countries laos  was split between royalists and  conservatives on the one hand and  communist rebels the potted lao on the  other  so along with the rest of indochina the  country entered a civil war  on its own the war would probably not  have been so bad the first years of the  conflict in fact were marked by relative  calm  but when larger and more important  vietnam descended into civil war in the  1960s and attracted international  involvement  laos became a proxy front in a much  bigger conflict  the united states  is therefore prepared to take all  necessary steps  in defense of freedom  the royalists governed a strip of land  in the west supported by american forces  based in thailand  the patatlao which ruled the north and  the east was propped up by its stronger  ally communist north vietnam  this had less to do with interest in  laos itself than with a question of  military logistics one of the biggest  targets of the vietnam era war was laos  some of the raids were aimed at  communist insurgents and others at the  ho chi minh trail the system of roads  that the enemy used to carry men and  supplies to the south  a big part of that trail ran through  eastern laos  as secretary of state dean rusk said  laos was only the wart on the hog  so the u.s planned a military campaign  to destroy the ho chi minh trail and  defeat the pathet law who are protecting  it they called it operation barrel roll  but there was something important  it would have to remain top secret no  mention of it was ever to be made to the  public not even to the united states  senate  it was called the secret war and was  operated not by the military but by the  cia  on the ground the us would supply a  warlord army composed of the country's  ethnic minorities  the war involved more than just nations  royalists and fiercely anti-communist  were often called the secret army  because they were supplied paid and  organized entirely by money and  officials from america's central  intelligence agency  the secret army was supplied by a  commercial airline called air america in  reality a cia front  the warlords used air america to smuggle  opium into vietnam in order to fund  their operations  and from above american planes would  attack at all hours of the day  washington never admitted the scale of  the bombing nor permitted film coverage  of it the only pictures released were  those showing rice supplies being  dropped to refugees  the scale of the bombing was enormous  [Music]  just look at this graphic created by  mother jones  [Music]  from 1964 to 1973 the united states  dropped more than 2 million tons of  bombs on laos  the u.s dropped more bombs just on laos  than it dropped during the entirety of  the second world war on both germany and  japan  a bomb was dropped an average of every  eight minutes 24 hours a day for nine  years  from bases in thailand huge b-52s flew  up to 300 sorties a day against  suspected communist positions many  innocent villagers were undoubtedly  killed or made homeless by the raids  the plane of jars in northern laos so  called because of its ancient megalithic  rock structures became the most  intensely bombed place on earth  in 1960 the plane of jars had a  population of 150 000 people by 1970  just 9 000 were left  of the 83 000 water buffalo in the  province  50 000 were killed by the bombings  to the peasants and tribes people of  laos the bombing campaign was like  something out of a nightmare one  villager said quote the planes came like  birds and the bombs fell like rain  soon northern and eastern laos looked  like the surface of the moon totally  barren pitch black  one u.s air force officer said quote  there just aren't any villages in  northern laos anymore  of the roughly ten thousand villages in  the country thirty five hundred were  held for the patatlao  of these the majority were obliterated  by bombing  [Music]  the girl employees say they are not  afraid of the enemy  they will willingly endure any hardship  no matter how long the fight goes on  there will be no happiness for anyone  until the united states is defeated  [Music]  it was not until the early 1970s that  people learned just how extensive the  american bombing of laos had been  the cia kept it such a close secret that  even leading senators were shocked to  find out that america had been bombing  for years  in the meantime an entire nation had  been bombed to the dust  but the surprising thing was that nobody  in america really cared  laos was not what got nixon in trouble  nobody in the military or the cia lost  their jobs  laos was an afterthought  and in 1975 after the bombing ended  the pathet lao managed to sweep to  victory  causing their rivals to flee to thailand  and america  [Music]  but the legacy of the bombing remained  powerful because 30 percent of the bombs  had never gone off so the landscape of  laos was still covered with unexploded  munitions which would detonate and kill  people when touched  in the decades since the bombing ended  thousands of lotions have been  permanently disfigured by bombs  there were so many bombs lying around  that some restaurants would use them as  decoration  schools put up warnings so that children  would know to avoid unexploded bombs  kids even earned money by dismantling  bomb scraps  since the 1970s about 20 000 lotions  have been killed by unexploded bombs  a massacre in slow motion  [Music]  about a quarter of villages in laos  still have unexploded munitions  40 of those killed were children  laos was once called the land of a  million elephants  now it's the land of a million  unexploded mobs  eight-year-old braun young has shrapnel  in his side he made the mistake of  playing with what he thought was a ball  instead it exploded  in 2016 the us government finally  admitted what it had done in laos  over the years thousands of laotians  have been killed or injured  farmers tending their fields  children playing and that's why as  president i've dramatically increased  our funding to help remove these  unexploded bombs  still what america did in laos remains  obscure  it's not taught in schools in america  it is totally forgotten  [Music]  and the destruction in laos did not  teach the american government any  lessons about how to treat other  countries in the future  our two governments  will continue to have differences that's  true with many nations  as we do around the world the united  states will continue to speak up on  behalf of what we consider universal  human rights including the rights  of the people allow us to express  yourselves freely and decide your own  future  in the 1960s and 70s laotians had wanted  to determine their own future among  themselves to determine what type of  country they would be  american bombing was designed to stop  that  and that pattern of force never really  stopped  in the decades after the american  strategy of overwhelming air power would  be used all over the world to intervene  in other countries  a strategy of shock and awe  and each time the worst consequences  would be born not by the fighters on any  side but by innocent people who did  nothing to deserve what came to them  [Music]  [Music]  you

